From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 09:01:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: add dynamic-power-coefficient, so an Energy
 Model exists

Set dynamic-power-coefficient on all eight cpu@N: 170 on the six
Cortex-A55, 512 on the two Cortex-A76. With it, cpufreq-dt registers an
Energy Model for both perf domains from the OPP table
(cpufreq_register_em_with_opp), and Energy Aware Scheduling can run:
the tree already has asymmetric capacities (capacity-dmips-mhz 454/1024)
and cpufreq on both clusters.

WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM. A wall-power fit on the 8 GiB board, bin 5: one core
pinned per OPP, the rest idle, P = C * f * V^2 over the 25 OPPs.
A55 C = 170 +- 4.2 (R^2 0.976), A76 C = 512 +- 7.8 (R^2 0.987), ratio
3.01. These are wall-referred and not calibrated: the supply's loss sits
in the numbers (read by hand at two points, P_board ~= 0.864 * P_wall -
0.44 W), mains moved by about 1 V, the outlet resolves 0.1 W, and the
A76 fit leaves a +19 mW per-core step the model has no term for. On
those grounds the measurement alone would not qualify as a DT source.
It was decided on 2026-09-30 to ship it anyway, and the reason
holds up: EAS compares the energy of candidate placements across the two
perf domains, so a factor common to both (the supply) changes no
decision; what decides placement is the ratio, and that is the quantity
the A/B below exercised. The absolute scale matters to the power
allocator thermal governor, which no thermal zone here uses (step_wise).

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES, AND WHY NOT THAT. The vendor BSP DT carries 162 /
598 (ratio 3.69), unchanged from cubie-aiot-v1.4.8 to 1.5.0, with no
provenance. It makes the A76 look relatively more expensive, so EAS
packs more work onto the A55. Measured on the same board, both models
against EAS off, in the same boots:
  - bursts of fixed work (100 ms / 1 s / 5 s, 1 and 2 threads, from idle
    and over a 10 % background): finish time EAS on/off 1.000 +- 2 % for
    both models, 12 pairs each; the work reaches an A76 after ~27 ms
    either way (measured with a small burst test program);
  - one busy thread and full load: no resolved difference;
  - sustained duty-cycled load, 8 threads at 30 %: throughput EAS on/off
    0.93 with these values, 0.79 with the vendor's (overnight
    2026-09-29/30).
This board is expected to carry sustained partial load, so the model
that packs less wins. The energy saving EAS buys at that load (a few
hundred mW at the wall) was not a criterion.

Assisted-by: Claude
